Interview Summary
Concept	Meaning
Process	Independent running program
Thread	Lightweight execution path inside a process
start()	Creates a new thread and then calls run()
run()	Contains the work to execute
Runnable	Defines the task
Thread	Executes the task
synchronized	Allows only one thread at a time into a critical section
Race Condition	Multiple threads modify shared data incorrectly
Deadlock	Threads wait forever for each other's locks
wait()	Releases lock and waits
notify()	Wakes a waiting thread
sleep()	Pauses execution but keeps any held locks
ExecutorService	Manages a pool of reusable threads
CompletableFuture	Modern asynchronous programming API
The one sentence to remember forever

Imagine your Java application as a restaurant:

Threads are the waiters doing work.
CPU cores are the kitchen stations where work gets done.
The OS scheduler decides which waiter gets a station next.
synchronized is the key to the pantry—only one waiter can use it at a time.
wait() is a waiter stepping aside until ingredients arrive.
notify() is the chef calling the waiter back.
A thread pool is a fixed team of waiters that keeps serving customers instead of hiring and firing new waiters for every order.

Once you see multithreading as multiple workers sharing the same restaurant, concepts like race conditions, locks, and thread pools become much more intuitive than memorizing API names.











































